   "source": [
    "## 5. Conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Question | Test | Result | Decision (alpha = 0.05) |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Does tip depend on total bill? | Pearson correlation | r = 0.675, p < 0.001 | Strong positive relationship: bigger bills get bigger tips |\n",
    "| Smokers vs non-smokers (tip %) | Welch t-test | 16.33% vs 15.93%, p = 0.677, 95% CI of difference (-1.48, 2.28) | No significant difference |\n",
    "| Men vs women (tip %) | Welch t-test | 15.77% vs 16.66%, p = 0.249 | No significant difference |\n",
    "| Tip % across days | ANOVA / Kruskal-Wallis | p = 0.470 / 0.604 | No significant difference |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Takeaways**\n",
    "- The tip amount is mainly driven by the bill size, while the *percentage* people tip is fairly stable (about 16%) across smoker status, sex and day.\n",
    "- The variables are not perfectly normal (Shapiro-Wilk), but the non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis test agrees with ANOVA, so the conclusions are reliable.\n",
    "- \"Fail to reject H0\" does not prove the groups are equal; it only means this sample has no strong evidence of a difference.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** only 243 bills from one restaurant, and the data is observational, so the results show association, not cause."
   ]
